WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.179 --> 00:00:01.849
Speaker 1: Let's go to a win from Indiana.

00:00:01.849 --> 00:00:02.849
Win?

00:00:02.849 --> 00:00:03.849
Go.

00:00:03.849 --> 00:00:05.689
Speaker 3: Hi, can you hear me all right?

00:00:05.689 --> 00:00:07.450
Speaker 1: Yeah, I can hear you fine.

00:00:07.450 --> 00:00:08.450
Speaker 3: Awesome.

00:00:08.450 --> 00:00:11.090
So I have a question on economics again.

00:00:11.090 --> 00:00:17.210
So I'm currently taking macroeconomics and
I've learned the effects of spending and saving.

00:00:17.210 --> 00:00:20.220
You mentioned a lot about the marginal propensity
to consume.

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:21.220
Yes.

00:00:21.220 --> 00:00:22.220
And the multiplier effect.

00:00:22.220 --> 00:00:28.100
The idea that if you stimulate the poor, that
leads to an increase in GDP and economic growth

00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:29.920
and whatnot.

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:32.529
But my question is.

00:00:32.529 --> 00:00:42.360
So my question is, if you so the theory is
that if you stimulate the or like demand rate

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:48.329
it, it assumes that prices are stickier, which
is our prices don't change in the long in

00:00:48.329 --> 00:00:50.280
the short term, I believe.

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:53.219
So do you think in the real world, prices
are really sticky?

00:00:53.219 --> 00:00:59.700
Speaker 1: It depends on the industry and
it depends on the time horizon.

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:02.980
So I mean, I'll give you an example.

00:01:02.980 --> 00:01:12.310
So like in the short term, if you take something
like gas prices, gas prices respond to demand

00:01:12.310 --> 00:01:18.720
and they also, as we're seeing right now,
gas prices respond to supply.

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:23.990
But if you think about, for example, the type
of demand changes that it takes to move gas

00:01:23.990 --> 00:01:25.100
prices.

00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:31.390
We had a pandemic where global air travel
reduced dramatically.

00:01:31.390 --> 00:01:35.700
People driving reduced dramatically and we
saw gas prices go down.

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:42.420
But for the most part, if we talk about some
stimulus, some demand side stimulus for the

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:48.229
lower middle class in the United States, the
amount by which their demand for gas will

00:01:48.229 --> 00:01:52.350
increase is not really going to have an impact
on gas prices.

00:01:52.350 --> 00:01:53.490
It's too modest.

00:01:53.490 --> 00:01:58.430
So that's an example of where the additional
money to be able to, you know, maybe take

00:01:58.430 --> 00:02:05.190
a road trip or whatever it will change the
sort of life of the person getting the money

00:02:05.190 --> 00:02:10.909
and now having more money for gas without
really changing the price of gas in a significant

00:02:10.909 --> 00:02:11.909
way.

00:02:11.909 --> 00:02:13.659
It's sort of like asymmetrical, if that makes
sense.

00:02:13.659 --> 00:02:18.769
So so I think the question is always of what
good or service and over what time period

00:02:18.769 --> 00:02:20.090
is what we have to ask.

00:02:20.090 --> 00:02:27.090
Speaker 3: I see so when we implement like
specific policies.

00:02:27.090 --> 00:02:33.309
We have to look which industry, right, and
then because some some demand side stimulus

00:02:33.309 --> 00:02:38.950
may be a negative at a disadvantage towards
some industry too.

00:02:38.950 --> 00:02:42.599
And with that, would that would you consider
that like good policy?

00:02:42.599 --> 00:02:48.379
Does the fact that it's affecting a different
industry, let's say the service industry or

00:02:48.379 --> 00:02:51.730
the industry that is not really like a necessity,
so well,

00:02:51.730 --> 00:02:55.010
Speaker 1: it's it's getting a little bit
muddled what you're saying.

00:02:55.010 --> 00:02:59.590
But I think what I think the question you're
sort of asking is if we think about demand

00:02:59.590 --> 00:03:05.180
side stimulus as just being good because it
gives people more money to spend on stuff,

00:03:05.180 --> 00:03:10.669
don't we also need to consider that increased
demand as a result of those people having

00:03:10.669 --> 00:03:14.739
more money can increase prices, and that might
not be so good.

00:03:14.739 --> 00:03:16.260
Speaker 3: Yeah, yeah.

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:17.400
Yeah, that's much.

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:18.400
Face it.

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:21.650
Speaker 1: You really have to look at it more
specifically.

00:03:21.650 --> 00:03:25.689
And you also have to remember that in traditional
economic thinking, a little bit of inflation

00:03:25.689 --> 00:03:27.109
is considered good.

00:03:27.109 --> 00:03:30.470
You don't want too much inflation and you
don't want deflation, and I've talked through

00:03:30.470 --> 00:03:32.949
the internal logic of that.

00:03:32.949 --> 00:03:37.650
So just because some demand side stimulus
will cause a little bit of inflation also

00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:38.809
doesn't mean it's bad.

00:03:38.809 --> 00:03:42.129
I apologize because there's really not one
answer to this.

00:03:42.129 --> 00:03:43.419
Speaker 3: Right, right, right.

00:03:43.419 --> 00:03:44.419
I agree.

00:03:44.419 --> 00:03:47.370
Speaker 1: But it sounds like you're generally
understanding the concept, which is the most

00:03:47.370 --> 00:03:48.370
important thing.

00:03:48.370 --> 00:03:50.939
Speaker 3: Yeah, because I'm in like this.

00:03:50.939 --> 00:03:56.460
I don't know, like the middle phase kind of
like, I don't know what to unders like, read

00:03:56.460 --> 00:03:57.460
or understand.

00:03:57.460 --> 00:03:59.919
And I'm just wondering they want to find the
truth to this.

00:03:59.919 --> 00:04:05.749
But there's again, there's a lot of economic
data backing up the demand side stimulus.

00:04:05.749 --> 00:04:07.359
I mean, demand side policy.

00:04:07.359 --> 00:04:08.389
Speaker 1: Yeah.

00:04:08.389 --> 00:04:12.329
I mean, listen, you're asked, you seem to
be bringing the right amount of skepticism

00:04:12.329 --> 00:04:14.629
to the things that you're learning, which
is fantastic.

00:04:14.629 --> 00:04:17.000
Speaker 3: Yeah, yeah.

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:18.000
Awesome.

00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:20.280
And one one really quick last question.

00:04:20.280 --> 00:04:22.019
Do you so?

00:04:22.019 --> 00:04:27.810
What are your thoughts on saving and economic
growth, because there's also some data on

00:04:27.810 --> 00:04:29.819
that, like countries that say war leads to.

00:04:29.819 --> 00:04:32.090
They tend to have a higher economic growth.

00:04:32.090 --> 00:04:33.169
What were your thoughts on that?

00:04:33.169 --> 00:04:37.430
Speaker 1: Well, so there's a difference between
the micro and the macro level.

00:04:37.430 --> 00:04:44.139
I mean, we've talked before about how given
the option of.

00:04:44.139 --> 00:04:51.070
Giving a tax cut to a very rich person or
a poor person, the rich person is more likely

00:04:51.070 --> 00:04:55.389
to just sock the money in a bank account because
they don't really need that money to live,

00:04:55.389 --> 00:04:59.479
whereas the poor person is more likely to
spend it and the spending has a higher multiplier

00:04:59.479 --> 00:05:01.289
than the saving in a bank account.

00:05:01.289 --> 00:05:03.979
So that's like the individual level.

00:05:03.979 --> 00:05:11.039
At the same time, saving at the macroeconomic
level, meaning how much is society saving

00:05:11.039 --> 00:05:18.370
in total is a sign of economic health because
it means you don't have a society with as

00:05:18.370 --> 00:05:23.599
many people on the brink of not being able
to afford basic expenses.

00:05:23.599 --> 00:05:29.280
So when we think about who do we want to target
for tax refunds or tax credits, we would have

00:05:29.280 --> 00:05:30.780
one answer to that.

00:05:30.780 --> 00:05:34.539
And we might say, well, just putting the money
in a bank account is not so good as in terms

00:05:34.539 --> 00:05:40.189
of a multiplier at the macroeconomic level,
a higher savings rate within reason is a sign

00:05:40.189 --> 00:05:41.319
of economic health.

00:05:41.319 --> 00:05:45.770
So the answer is different depending on whether
you're talking about macro or micro.

00:05:45.770 --> 00:05:52.039
Speaker 3: OK, OK, because but if we're targeting,
if you want for the benefit of society, you

00:05:52.039 --> 00:05:54.569
know, obviously GDP is not everything, right?

00:05:54.569 --> 00:05:57.690
No, I'd like for the benefit of society then.

00:05:57.690 --> 00:06:02.960
Probably tax cuts for the for the wealthy
isn't really the best option currently.

00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:04.409
Speaker 1: That's what the data show.

00:06:04.409 --> 00:06:06.030
Speaker 3: Yeah, OK.

00:06:06.030 --> 00:06:07.030
OK.

00:06:07.030 --> 00:06:08.030
Got it.

00:06:08.030 --> 00:06:09.030
All right.

00:06:09.030 --> 00:06:10.030
Thank you so much, David.

00:06:10.030 --> 00:06:11.030
All right.

00:06:11.030 --> 00:06:12.030
Speaker 1: Thanks.

00:06:12.030 --> 00:06:14.389
When we'll win with some of the meatiest economics
questions we've had in a while.

00:06:14.389 --> 00:06:16.410
I absolutely love it.

00:06:16.410 --> 00:06:17.410
All right, everybody.

00:06:17.410 --> 00:06:18.410
We're going to go to a break.

00:06:18.410 --> 00:06:20.439
